            <ArticleTitle>Chest wall and rib irradiation and toxicities of early-stage lung cancer patients treated with CyberKnife stereotactic body radiotherapy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2311-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2217/fon.14.158</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of the study is to evaluate the chest wall and rib toxicities in primary lung cancer patients treated with CyberKnife-based stereotactic body radiotherapy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS &amp; METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In this study, data were collected from the 118 patients, of which 25 patients who had longer follow-up (mean: 21.9 months) were considered. Studied parameters were maximum point dose, doses to 1-100 cm(3) of chest wall and 1-10 cm(3) of ribs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Three patients developed chest wall pain (grade I). 25 studied patients, on average, received 27.7 Gy to 30 cm(3) of chest wall and 50.4 Gy to 1 cm(3) of rib. Nine patients had more than 30 Gy dose to 30 cm(3) of chest wall. No rib bone fracture was found.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">No correlations of chest wall pain and volume of irradiation were found.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
